timestamp=2026-11-09T10:32:47.100Z level=INFO run=195d671c message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# The JSON only has calibrations up to FARM_X (24 farms). But we have production files for Y,Z,AA..AH (10 more files) and also maybe there's a different calibration structure? Or perhaps the calibration info for the remaining farms is embedded somewhere else - or maybe the value column name is directly in the file structure? Or look at other common fields.\n# Also look at the full production data for these farms to understand what the \"value\" is that contributes to energy. For power (kW) readings over time, energy might be integral of power over time interval? Or maybe the \"reading\" is already energy in Wh/kWh etc? The task says \"real energy production in kilowatt-hours (kWh)\" - so the values need to be interpreted as energy readings over intervals, and we sum them up for the day.\n# Let us also look up the units mentioned in sensors_calibration.json for known farms: e.g. FARM_A unit is KwH (kWh). FARM_B MwH, etc. That gives us clues. But missing for others.\n# Maybe look at the file headers or more data for each extra farm? Also check for any other JSON/CSV that might contain calibration?\n# Let us search for \"calibration\" or \"FARM_Y\" etc in all files.\nimport sys\nfrom pathlib import Path\np = Path('/app/dataset')\nfor f in p.iterdir():\n    if f.is_file():\n        try:\n            content = f.read_text(encoding='utf-8', errors='ignore')\n        except:\n            try:\n                content = f.read_text(encoding='utf-16le', errors='ignore')\n            except:\n                continue\n        if 'FARM_Y' in content or 'FARM_AA' in content or 'calibration_offset' in content and any(idf in content for idf in ['FARM_Y','FARM_Z','FARM_AA']):\n            print('FOUND in', f.name)\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
