timestamp=2026-11-09T10:34:45.227Z level=INFO run=195d671c message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# They have substantial data. But they don't map to the 24-farm registry. Also the task says \"fleet of 24 solar photovoltaic farms\" and gives files for exactly those? Let us look at the full set of production files listed in the task - wait let us count again. In the task it lists production_FARM_A.csv through production_FARM_AH.csv. That is A to AH. Counting A(1) to Z(26) gives A-Z = 26 files, then AA,AB,AC,AD,AE,AF,AG,AH = 8 more, so 26+8=34 total. But only 24 farms in fleet. That is confusing. Maybe we need to compute for ALL farms that have production files and calibration? Or maybe the \"24 farms\" are the ones with complete registry + calibration?\n# Let us look at how many unique farm_ids we have total. Also maybe the answer should include all farm_ids found? Let us check the example in answer format - they show FARM_A, FARM_B, FARM_C etc as examples. Maybe compute for every production file that has a corresponding farm_id and calibration in the standard set (A-X)? Or maybe for all farm_ids that appear as FARM_* and for which we have data? Let us look at what others might expect - but we need to infer. Another interpretation: the 24 farms are the ones listed in farms.csv (A-X). The extra production files (Y,Z,AA..AH) might be for those same 24 farms but in different formats? Or maybe typos/misnamed? That seems unlikely given the scale. Or maybe they are additional farms - but then we don't have their metadata. Let us look at the full production data for one of these extra files to understand units/meaning.\npass\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
