timestamp=2026-11-09T10:56:56.159Z level=INFO run=195d671c message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# So FARM_A has data. Let us look for timestamps on 2024-03-15. Also Europe/Warsaw is UTC+1 in winter? Or check offset. But regardless, we filter by UTC window [2024-03-15 00:00:00 UTC, 2024-03-16 00:00:00 UTC) - so we need all local readings whose UTC timestamps fall in that window.\n# Let us look at what UTC times we get for FARM_A around end of 14th and start of 15th? For example, 2024-03-14 23:30:00 in Europe/Warsaw (CET, UTC+1) would be 22:30:00 UTC on 14th; or during DST? March 2024 in Europe - Poland is on CEST (UTC+2) from last Sunday of March. March 31 2024 is start? Or March 10? Actually EU daylight saving starts last Sunday in March - March 31, 2024. So on 2024-03-15, Europe/Warsaw is still UTC+1 (CET) before the change in some regions? Wait last Sunday of March 2024 is March 31. So DST starts March 31, 2024 - clocks go forward 1 hour. So on March 15, Warsaw is UTC+1 (CET). That means 00:00:00 2024-03-15 local is 23:00:00 2024-03-14 UTC. So the local day 2024-03-15 starts at 23:00:00 2024-03-14 UTC and ends at 23:00:00 2024-03-15 UTC. Our target window is [2024-03-15 00:00:00 UTC, 2024-03-16 00:00:00 UTC) - that corresponds to local times roughly 01:00:00 2024-03-15 to 01:00:00 2024-03-16 in Warsaw (UTC+1). So we need to include readings from that local period.\npass\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
