timestamp=2026-11-09T10:58:51.353Z level=INFO run=195d671c message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# FARM_B: value column is 'value', unit is MwH (energy). So values are energy readings in MwH. For interval energy readings, summing intervals over the day gives total energy for the day. That matches - so we sum them. Also after applying calibration offset and dropping negatives. Let us look at how negatives are handled. The rule says \"Any value that is negative must be dropped, this rule is valid only after the calibration offset is applied.\"\n# So for FARM_B, calibration_offset is -0.04 (from JSON). So true value = recorded value - calibration_offset = recorded value - (-0.04) = recorded value + 0.04. That turns small negative recorded values into positive true values. That affects the result. Our current implementation does: value_true = value_raw - offset (where offset is calibration_offset). Yes, that matches \"calibration_offset is defined as: (recorded value) - (true value)\". So true = recorded - offset. Correct.\n# Also converting units: FARM_B unit is MwH, so total_kwh = total_energy_raw_units_converted? Wait look at our convert_energy_to_kwh: for MwH it returns value * 1000.0, so if total is in MwH units, that gives kWh. Correct.\n# Let us recompute with the correct logic. But is our current logic summing interval energy correctly? That is correct. Another case: for power readings (reading_type power), we need to integrate over time intervals to get energy. That is missing in our current implementation!\npass\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
