timestamp=2026-11-09T11:22:51.993Z level=INFO run=195d671c message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# Good, now we have power_reading and power_reading_1 for FARM_D. But which one is correct? Based on calibration offset 80.0 and the values, the correct column is likely power_reading_1 (the unspaced one in original gave 0.0s, and also power_reading gave 80.0s). Wait - earlier when we looked at the original by position: col 15 (power_reading with space) had 80.0, col 16 (power_reading without space) had 0.0. So after normalization and numbering in order of appearance, the first occurrence is the spaced one - power_reading becomes the first (index 15), power_reading_1 becomes the second (index 16) with value 0.0. Also calibration value_column_name was \"power_reading \" (with space) and offset was 80.0. If recorded is 80.0, then true = recorded - offset = 0.0. That means all true values would be 0.0 - which can't be. That can't be right for a solar farm producing energy during daylight.\n# Or if recorded is 0.0 (power_reading_1) and offset is 80.0, then true = recorded - offset = -80.0. After dropping negatives, everything is dropped → total is 0. Also not right. Or if true = recorded + offset? Wait check the definition again: \"The calibration_offset is defined as: (recorded value) - (true value).\" So true = recorded - offset. That is correct. But which recorded value matches reality? Maybe the second column (power_reading_1) contains the actual recorded measurements and the first is something else like a baseline. Let us look at other farms with similar structure. Also let us look at the actual magnitude of power readings in the second column during daylight for FARM_D.\nfrom read_production import read_production\nfrom utils import parse_numeric\n\ndf = read_production('FARM_D')\n# look at power_reading_1 values\nvals = df['power_reading_1'].apply(parse_numeric)\n# see some non-zero\nfor i, v in enumerate(vals):\n    if v > 0:\n        print(f'{i}: {v}')\n        if i > 5: break\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
