timestamp=2026-11-09T11:23:52.392Z level=INFO run=195d671c message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# power_reading_1 has more reasonable solar power values (hundreds of kW range with variation). power_reading is oddly large/constant-like baseline. So the real measurement is power_reading_1. That means for calibration value_column_name \"power_reading \" (spaced), the corresponding data column in our parsed structure is the one with the actual measurements? Or maybe the calibration is pointing to the wrong column? But we need to follow the specification as given. However, the calibration says value_column_name - let us look at other farms. For example, FARM_F also had duplicate output columns. Let us check FARM_F's values.\nfrom read_production import read_production\nfrom utils import parse_numeric\n\ndf = read_production('FARM_F')\nfor col in [c for c in df.columns if 'output' in c]:\n    vals = df[col].apply(parse_numeric)\n    print(f'{col}: non-nan={vals.notna().sum()}, mean={vals.mean():.2f}, std={vals.std():.2f}, max={vals.max():.2f}')\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
