timestamp=2026-11-09T11:28:55.525Z level=INFO run=195d671c message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# Let us look at some of the numbers. For power farms, integrating over intervals gives much more reasonable values (FARM_D went from 1.86M to 155K, FARM_O went from 1.02M to ~11.5K). That looks plausible. \n# Also check maintenance: we filtered COMPLETED maintenance windows. For example, \"Scheduled cleaning at Vistula Photovoltaics on March 15, starting at 12:00 and finishing at 14:00 local time. Status: COMPLETED.\" - that affects FARM_F (Vistula Photovoltaics). Did that reduce FARM_F? Possibly. That’s correct.\n# Also Sakura Energy Site had a COMPLETED firmware update on 16th March and CANCELLED inspection on 15th. The 16th is outside our target UTC window (2024-03-15 UTC day), so it shouldn’t affect. The cancelled one doesn’t affect. That’s correct.\n# Now let us look at the remaining huge value: FARM_T is 1,777,788,845 which is still extremely large. That suggests either it’s a power reading that needs integration, or unit conversion issue, or the value column/reading type interpretation is off.\n# Let us examine FARM_T details.\nimport json\nwith open('/app/dataset/sensors_calibration.json') as f:\n    data = json.load(f)\nfarms = data['enterprise_platform']['deployment_environments']['system_config']['farms']\nif 'FARM_T' in farms:\n    print(farms['FARM_T'])\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
