timestamp=2026-11-09T11:37:41.445Z level=INFO run=195d671c message=evaluated permission=bash pattern="python3 << 'PY'\n# The numbers now look much more plausible. Let us compare against typical solar farm daily yields - MW-scale farms producing 10^3 to 10^4 kWh per day depending on capacity and conditions. These values seem reasonable. Also we filtered out the 888888 sentinel values which were causing huge totals.\n# Let us also think about other potential issues: \"Duplicates should be handled with pandas drop_duplicates()\" - we already do this. \"Any value that is negative must be dropped, this rule is valid only after the calibration offset is applied\" - we do this correctly. \"Readings recorded during successfully conducted maintenance windows must be excluded\" - we parse maintenance and exclude COMPLETED windows; seems correct.\n# Also \"If a reading's timestamp cannot be parsed reliably as a valid date and time, that reading must be ignored\" - we already skip unparseable timestamps.\n# Let us look at the full set and see if any values look odd. For example, some farms give 0 - like FARM_G, FARM_I, FARM_K etc. That could be correct if there’s no valid data in the reporting window, or if all values become negative after offset and get dropped. Let us quickly sanity check one such case, e.g. FARM_G.\n# FARM_G: reading_type is power (Kw), calibration_offset is 120.0, value_col is generation. So true = recorded - offset; if recorded values are small, true becomes negative and gets dropped. That’s possible. Similarly for others.\n# The values overall seem reasonable now. Let us generate the final answer files as specified.\npass\nPY" action.permission=* action.action=allow action.pattern=*
